## Meta-Identity
Epistemological foundation for investigative frameworks. Explains why community is structurally necessary, how collective knowledge emerges from distributed observation, and when to trust convergent findings versus acknowledge uncertainty. Grounds humility without enabling relativism.

---

## Core Premise: Knowledge-Space Exceeds Individual Capacity

### Structural Limit, Not Moral Failure
- Knowledge-space (all possible observations about reality) infinitely exceeds finite consciousness
- Individual perspective occupies particular position, observes from specific angle
- Like a bird in flock: each sees different region simultaneously
- No single position offers complete view

### Why This Matters
- Eliminates "omniscient observer" fantasy
- Grounds appropriate humility in structure of cognition itself
- Makes "I don't know" epistemologically honest, not weakness
- Explains why ancient peoples were accurate within their observational range while being wrong about things outside it

